\textbf{\textcolor{red}{MODELLING TAXATION (HSV FRAMEWORK).}}

Gov spends each year  G (defense, healthcare...)
\[ G = \int T(y)\, df(y) \;\;\;\;\textbf{[A]} \quad\quad\text{Totally diff than before.} \]\[ T(y) = y - c, \quad \text{where} \;\; \boxed{\;c \;=\; B\, y^{1-\tau},\;\; B > 0,\;\; \tau \in [0, 1].\;} \]
\emph{So planner chooses $B, \tau$ to satisfy [A]. Note: if you choose $\tau$, $B$ needs to adjust to have G.}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Reading $\tau$ (progressivity dial).}}
$\bullet$ \emph{If $\tau = 0$} (0 progressivity), tax is indep.\ of income: $c = B y$ (flat tax, \% of $y$).
$\bullet$ \emph{If $\tau > 0$}, $c < y$ $\to$ \textbf{progressive tax} (rich pay an higher %) 
$\bullet$ \emph{$\tau = 1$}: total tax, $c = B$, \emph{we all consume the same amount} (communism). Hyper progressivity: the rich pays so much he gets to the poor level of consumption.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{EXTERNALITIES --- setup and the planner's problem.}}

\textbf{Def.} Agent $i$ optimises his consumption based \emph{only on his own MU}, \emph{not accounting for spillovers to other agents}.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{0. Setting.}} Quasi-linear utilities with a shared good $y$ and a private numeraire $x$:
\[ U^i(x^i, y^1, y^2) \;=\; U^i(y^1, y^2) + x^i,\;\; i = 1, 2. \]
Here $U^i$ depends on \emph{both} agents' $y$ --- the spillover is the off-own argument.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{1. Planner's problem  $P(\bar U)$.}}
\[\;P(\bar U) \;=\; \max_{y, x}\; U^1(y^1, y^2) + x^1\;\;\text{sub.}\;\]
$\bullet$ Resource constraint on $y$: $y^1 + y^2 \le \omega_y^1 + \omega_y^2$ \;\;($\mu_y$) \quad [1]

$\bullet$ Resource constraint on $x$: $x^1 + x^2 \le \omega_x^1 + \omega_x^2$ \;\;($\mu_x$) \quad [2]

$\bullet$ Pareto participation for agent 2: $U^2(y^1, y^2) + x^2 \ge \bar U$ \;\;($\lambda$) \quad [3]

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{2. Lagrangian.}}
\[ \mathcal{L} \;=\; U^1(y^1, y^2) + x^1 + \mu_y\bigl(\omega_y - y^1 - y^2\bigr) + \mu_x\bigl(\omega_x - x^1 - x^2\bigr) + \lambda\bigl(U^2(y^1, y^2) + x^2 - \bar U\bigr). \]

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{3. FOCs.}}

$\bullet$ $\partial \mathcal{L}/\partial x^1: \; 1 - \mu_x = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; \mu_x = 1$. \;\;[A]

$\bullet$ $\partial \mathcal{L}/\partial x^2: \; -\mu_x + \lambda = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; \;\lambda = \mu_x = 1.\ $ \;\;[B]

$\bullet$ $\partial \mathcal{L}/\partial y^1: \; \dfrac{\partial U^1}{\partial y^1} + \lambda\,\dfrac{\partial U^2}{\partial y^1} - \mu_y = 0$.

$\bullet$ $\partial \mathcal{L}/\partial y^2: \; \dfrac{\partial U^1}{\partial y^2} + \lambda\,\dfrac{\partial U^2}{\partial y^2} - \mu_y = 0$.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{4. Personal vs social MU.}}
Use $\lambda = 1$ from [B]:
\[
\boxed{\;\;
\underbrace{\frac{\partial U^1}{\partial y^1}}_{\text{personal cons.}}
+
\underbrace{\frac{\partial U^2}{\partial y^1}}_{\textcolor{red}{\text{extern.}\;(<0)}}
=
\mu_y,
\qquad
\underbrace{\frac{\partial U^1}{\partial y^2}}_{\textcolor{red}{\text{extern.}\;(<0)}}
+
\underbrace{\frac{\partial U^2}{\partial y^2}}_{\text{personal cons.}}
=
\mu_y.
\;\;}
\]
Consider the pre-post the externality introudction: 
- 
- now that we add a negative value to the old one
\[
\boxed{\;\;
\underbrace{\frac{\partial U^1}{\partial y^1}}_{\text{personal cons.}}
+
\underbrace{\frac{\partial U^2}{\partial y^1}}_{\textcolor{red}{\text{extern.}\;(<0)}}
=
\mu_y,
\;\;}\]
Note that this is leading agents to stop consuming "earlier"

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Reading the externality FOCs --- when constraints bind / good is destroyed.}}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{① Can $\mu_y$ be $0$? (Goods destroyed.)}}

Put [A]\,$\mu_x = 1$ and [B]\,$\lambda = 1$ into the resource constraint [1]:
\[ \text{if } \omega_y > [\cdot] \;\Rightarrow\; \mu_y > 0,\qquad \text{otherwise } \mu_y = 0. \]
\emph{Reading $\mu_y = 0$:} the marginal social value of one extra unit of $y$ is zero $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{goods are better kept idle} (rejected by the planner!) when the spillover wipes out the direct benefit.

\textbf{Three ways $\mu_y = 0$ can arise:}

$\bullet$ \emph{Corner with full transfer to agent 2.} $y^1 = 0$ (non-interior) and $A = \mu_y^1 = B$: \textbf{agent 2 consumes all}.

$\bullet$ \emph{Both A,B $= 0$.} Agent 1 is depleted when his MU $\to 0$ but agent 2 is no longer absorbing because his $U$ is saturated. \emph{The only thing that can stop him is himself.}

$\bullet$ \emph{Both A,B $< 0$.} Negative spillover dominates the direct utility $\Rightarrow$ planner destroys units (puts $y^i$ in the bin).

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{② Can [1] not bind? $\mu_y = 0$ a priori.}}

\textcolor{purple}{\emph{$\omega_y$ will pin down $\mu_y$.}} Substitute [A], [B] in [1] $\to$ get $y^i$. Then to get $x^i$: if $U$ is quasi-linear, $x^i$ is \emph{not} in the FOC $\to$ use [3].

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{③ Special case  $A = C$ (agent 2 inelastic on $C$).}}
Hence price $P\uparrow$ by $MC\uparrow$ \emph{or some intermediate value} --- the shadow price $\downarrow$ by $\downarrow\downarrow$.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Effect on $x$.}} Trivial budget logic: \emph{agent 1 has stuck some $y$ $\to$ uses the money to buy $x$. Agent 2 is the opposite.}

\textcolor{purple}{\emph{Remark.}} Agents are price takers. But \textbf{they don't know they affect prices}. Still, \emph{ex-post} they may observe $AS = AD$.

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Cross-refs:}} planner setup, QL/CD examples, Pigou remedies.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Timeline --- from CE to the planner's reallocation.}}

\textbf{① $MB$ shifts down by $MC\uparrow$.}

Once the spillover is internalised, the agent who \emph{causes} the externality sees his \emph{effective} MB curve shift down by the marginal harm to the other agent (i.e.\ by $|\partial U^j/\partial y^i|$).

\textbf{② Planner now sees that giving these units to agent 1 is less convenient than giving them to agent 2.}

So she reallocates:
