\textcolor{purple}{\emph{Net effect at CE post-trade vs.\ planner pre-trade: the externality wedge ($R$, in red).}}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Reading the diagram.}}

$\bullet$ \emph{Left panel.} $MB^1$ shifts down by the externality $\to$ new intersection with $MB^2$ at a lower aggregate $y$ and a different split. The \textcolor{red}{red wedge} between old and new is the externality $R$.

$\bullet$ \emph{Right panel.} Aggregate quantity sold (the planner puts a \emph{quota} or trades in the private-bad market). \emph{Note how the wedge $R$ is larger when the externality is more severe (poll/social loss).}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Post-trade comparison.}}

$\bullet$ \emph{Pre-trade planner $\equiv$ CE} only if no externality.

$\bullet$ \emph{Post-trade planner $<$ CE} on the externality-causing agent's consumption.

\textcolor{purple}{\emph{Price-taker remark.}} CE agents are price takers $\to$ they don't realise they can affect prices. \emph{Ex post}, market clearing $AS = AD$ still holds at the inefficient allocation.

